@title How a Water-Damage Claim Moves Through the Company
@subtitle Where claims wait, where we redo work, and what the rework costs. Source: Q3 claims operations dashboard.
@author Operations Analysis

A homeowner's water-damage claim (a burst pipe, a leaking roof) passes through ten steps, and each step belongs to a different team. The waiting comes from hand-offs between teams, from homeowners who have to be home for an inspection, and from contractor estimates that disagree. This page shows the path a claim takes, how long each step takes, and what it costs us when a claim has to be sent back and done again.

# The words you will see

table = term,what it means; First notice of loss (FNOL),"The customer's first report that damage happened, by phone, app, or through their agent."; Coverage check,"Confirming the policy was active and covers this kind of loss. A lapsed policy (premium unpaid) or a “non-owner occupied” one (the owner does not live there, for example a rental) needs extra review."; Adjuster,"The employee who decides what we owe. Field adjusters visit the home; desk adjusters work from reports, photos and contractor quotes."; Estimate & scope,"The written list of what must be repaired (the scope) and what it will cost (the estimate)."; Supervisor approval,"A second sign-off, required when the payout would be more than $10,000."; Fraud screen,"A check for signs of a false or inflated claim. Suspicious claims go to Special investigations for a full review."; Mitigation,"Emergency work to stop the damage getting worse, such as pumping out water and drying the structure, done by outside vendors."; Sent back (rework),"A claim returned to an earlier step because something was missing or wrong. Every send-back costs staff time a second time."; Reopened,"A claim we closed that the customer comes back to, here within 90 days."; Median,"The middle claim: half of claims finish the step faster than this, half take longer."; 90th percentile (90th pct),"The slow tail: 9 in 10 claims finish the step within this time, and 1 in 10 takes longer."

